WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:01.965
- Yeah, that was pretty good, right?

00:00:01.965 --> 00:00:04.318
It's like I'm high right
now, it's really hot,

00:00:04.318 --> 00:00:05.377
I don't know how you do it, man.

00:00:05.377 --> 00:00:07.423
Maybe, just, I have,
I don't, I'm not even.

00:00:07.423 --> 00:00:08.608
Alright, here we go.

00:00:08.608 --> 00:00:10.902
Too much hair product in my hair, mmkay.

00:00:10.902 --> 00:00:12.017
Good morning, Believe Nation.

00:00:12.017 --> 00:00:14.291
My name is Aleks, and
my one word is belong,

00:00:14.291 --> 00:00:15.840
and I believe the world
would be a better place

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:17.657
if more people felt that they belong.

00:00:17.657 --> 00:00:19.385
So to help you on your journey,

00:00:19.385 --> 00:00:22.222
today's message is be
a blessing to somebody.

00:00:22.222 --> 00:00:23.823
Over to you, Maya Angelou.

00:00:23.823 --> 00:00:26.362
(rooster crows)

00:00:26.362 --> 00:00:28.930
♫ I wake up every mornin'

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:31.645
♫ Espresso keep me goin'

00:00:31.645 --> 00:00:35.577
♫ I wake up every mornin'

00:00:35.577 --> 00:00:39.679
- There's an African-American
song, 19th century

00:00:39.679 --> 00:00:41.929
which is so great, it says,

00:00:44.806 --> 00:00:47.636
♫ When it look like the sun

00:00:47.636 --> 00:00:51.234
♫ Wasn't going to shine anymore

00:00:51.234 --> 00:00:56.198
♫ God put a rainbow in the clouds

00:00:56.198 --> 00:00:57.441
Imagine.

00:00:57.441 --> 00:01:00.973
And I've had so many
rainbows in my clouds.

00:01:00.973 --> 00:01:02.806
I had a lot of clouds.

00:01:04.033 --> 00:01:06.561
But I have had so many rainbows.

00:01:06.561 --> 00:01:08.308
And one of the things I do

00:01:08.308 --> 00:01:10.067
when I step up on the stage,

00:01:10.067 --> 00:01:12.907
when I step up to translate,

00:01:12.907 --> 00:01:15.498
when I go to teach my classes,

00:01:15.498 --> 00:01:19.681
when I go to direct a
movie, I bring everyone

00:01:19.681 --> 00:01:23.442
who has ever been kind to me with me.

00:01:23.442 --> 00:01:27.307
Black, white, Asian,
Spanish-speaking, Native-American,

00:01:27.307 --> 00:01:30.571
gay, straight, everybody,
I say, come with me,

00:01:30.571 --> 00:01:31.793
I'm going on the stage.

00:01:31.793 --> 00:01:33.472
Come with me, I need you now.

00:01:33.472 --> 00:01:35.055
Long dead, you see.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:40.000
So I don't ever feel I have no help.

00:01:41.683 --> 00:01:44.938
I've had rainbows in my clouds.

00:01:44.938 --> 00:01:47.938
And the thing to do, it seems to me,

00:01:48.847 --> 00:01:51.582
is to prepare yourself so that you can be

00:01:51.582 --> 00:01:54.238
a rainbow in somebody else's cloud.

00:01:54.238 --> 00:01:56.664
Somebody who may not look like you.

00:01:56.664 --> 00:01:59.438
May not call God the
same name you call God

00:01:59.438 --> 00:02:01.726
(laughing) if they call God at all.

00:02:01.726 --> 00:02:03.165
You see.

00:02:03.165 --> 00:02:06.833
And may not eat the same
dishes prepared the way you do.

00:02:06.833 --> 00:02:09.116
May not dance your dances.

00:02:09.116 --> 00:02:11.606
Or speak your language.

00:02:11.606 --> 00:02:14.917
But be a blessing to somebody.

00:02:14.917 --> 00:02:15.965
(whoosh)

00:02:15.965 --> 00:02:18.425
- This message resonates
with me as a human being,

00:02:18.425 --> 00:02:22.644
as an instructor, as a
speaker, as a leader.

00:02:22.644 --> 00:02:25.853
It is super important that
you take responsibility,

00:02:25.853 --> 00:02:29.079
not only for yourself, but
for the well-being of others.

00:02:29.079 --> 00:02:30.481
Because ultimately you never know

00:02:30.481 --> 00:02:32.908
what kind of impact you
could have on others.

00:02:32.908 --> 00:02:35.319
When I was a student, seven years ago,

00:02:35.319 --> 00:02:37.577
I struggled asking women to dance.

00:02:37.577 --> 00:02:39.922
So here was I, sort of a beginner dancer,

00:02:39.922 --> 00:02:42.246
going to socials, and
just being too afraid

00:02:42.246 --> 00:02:44.235
to mess up, make mistakes, and people

00:02:44.235 --> 00:02:46.007
not wanting to dance with me.

00:02:46.007 --> 00:02:47.709
And I remember there was a short woman,

00:02:47.709 --> 00:02:51.458
she was like 5' 1", 5
foot, she came up to me,

00:02:51.458 --> 00:02:53.241
she was an advanced
dancer, she had no reason

00:02:53.241 --> 00:02:54.679
really, to ask me to dance.

00:02:54.679 --> 00:02:56.322
But she saw how afraid I was,

00:02:56.322 --> 00:02:58.756
and she offered to dance with me.

00:02:58.756 --> 00:03:00.915
And for multiple socials
as I kept going out,

00:03:00.915 --> 00:03:01.917
she would always ask me to dance.

00:03:01.917 --> 00:03:03.322
And every time I'd get nervous,

00:03:03.322 --> 00:03:05.138
I'd make mistakes, I'd be hard on myself,

00:03:05.138 --> 00:03:06.622
I'd start looking around, I'm looking

00:03:06.622 --> 00:03:08.356
at all these other people dancing,

00:03:08.356 --> 00:03:11.395
and that being intimidating for me,

00:03:11.395 --> 00:03:12.854
she would tell me that it's okay.

00:03:12.854 --> 00:03:14.012
That it's not important.

00:03:14.012 --> 00:03:17.226
Just keep dancing, smiling,
that I'll get better.

00:03:17.226 --> 00:03:20.272
I saw her about six
years later at a social,

00:03:20.272 --> 00:03:22.066
by this point, I was
already an instructor.

00:03:22.066 --> 00:03:23.408
When I saw her at one of our socials,

00:03:23.408 --> 00:03:25.088
I ran up and I gave her a hug,

00:03:25.088 --> 00:03:27.263
and I told her how much
of a difference and impact

00:03:27.263 --> 00:03:29.897
she made on me, on my confidence,

00:03:29.897 --> 00:03:33.047
and was one of the pivotal
reasons why I stuck around.

00:03:33.047 --> 00:03:34.289
Even though at the beginning,

00:03:34.289 --> 00:03:36.292
I was one of the worst dancers.

00:03:36.292 --> 00:03:40.270
Never underestimate how powerful a simple

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:45.172
hello, a thank you, a you're
going a great job can be.

00:03:45.172 --> 00:03:48.494
Every single day is an opportunity to make

00:03:48.494 --> 00:03:51.484
a positive impact, in
the smallest of gestures

00:03:51.484 --> 00:03:53.324
in people's lives.

00:03:53.324 --> 00:03:54.705
And all it can sometimes take,

00:03:54.705 --> 00:03:57.031
is just one second of you stopping,

00:03:57.031 --> 00:03:59.314
and doing what you feel is right,

00:03:59.314 --> 00:04:02.141
versus walking away and
not wanting to deal with

00:04:02.141 --> 00:04:04.260
the hassle of things.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:07.077
Take responsibility,
not only for yourself,

00:04:07.077 --> 00:04:09.170
but take responsibility for others,

00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:11.897
and be a rainbow in somebody's cloud.

00:04:11.897 --> 00:04:12.730
(whooshes)

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:13.574
The question of the day is,

00:04:13.574 --> 00:04:16.610
what's one regret that you have right now

00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:18.107
where you wish you could've been a rainbow

00:04:18.107 --> 00:04:20.614
in someone else's cloud,
but you didn't take action?

00:04:20.614 --> 00:04:21.615
Leave it in the comments below,

00:04:21.615 --> 00:04:23.488
I'm excited to see what you have to say.

00:04:23.488 --> 00:04:25.524
I want to give a quick
shoutout to Emma Hayward,

00:04:25.524 --> 00:04:27.446
Emma thank you so much for
purchasing Evan's book,

00:04:27.446 --> 00:04:28.791
it really means a lot to us.

00:04:28.791 --> 00:04:30.814
I'm really curious when you
figure out your one word,

00:04:30.814 --> 00:04:33.024
and when you do, leave
it in the comments below,

00:04:33.024 --> 00:04:34.449
with your answer.

00:04:34.449 --> 00:04:35.517
Thank you so much for watching,

00:04:35.517 --> 00:04:37.380
we believe in you, and
we hope you continue

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:40.577
to believe in yourself and
whatever your one word is.

00:04:40.577 --> 00:04:41.817
And we'll see you tomorrow morning

00:04:41.817 --> 00:04:44.140
for another shot of entspresso.

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:46.774
♫ I wake up every mornin'

00:04:46.774 --> 00:04:50.297
♫ Espresso keep me goin'

00:04:50.297 --> 00:04:53.464
(typewriter clacking)

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:27.053
- I guess the advantage

00:05:27.053 --> 00:05:30.009
that my generation has,
when we were in film school,

00:05:30.009 --> 00:05:31.287
and we're starting in the film business,

00:05:31.287 --> 00:05:32.829
the door was absolutely locked.

00:05:32.829 --> 00:05:34.762
And it was a very, very high wall.

00:05:34.762 --> 00:05:36.634
And nobody got in.

00:05:36.634 --> 00:05:39.162
Therefore, all of us...

00:05:39.162 --> 00:05:43.523
Beggars and scroungers
down at the front gate

00:05:43.523 --> 00:05:46.905
decided that if we didn't
sort of band together,

00:05:46.905 --> 00:05:48.527
we wouldn't survive.

00:05:48.527 --> 00:05:50.356
And that if one could make it,

00:05:50.356 --> 00:05:51.909
that one would help
all the others make it.

00:05:51.909 --> 00:05:54.035
And we would continue to help each other.

00:05:54.035 --> 00:05:55.081
So we banded together.

00:05:55.081 --> 00:05:57.914
That's how cavemen figured it out.

00:05:59.095 --> 00:06:00.807
Any society starts that way,

00:06:00.807 --> 00:06:02.787
any society begins by realizing

00:06:02.787 --> 00:06:04.846
that together, by helping each other,

00:06:04.846 --> 00:06:08.478
you can survive better than
if you fight each other,

00:06:08.478 --> 00:06:09.916
and compete with each other.

00:06:09.916 --> 00:06:12.023
If I got a job, I would help
somebody else get a job.

00:06:12.023 --> 00:06:13.838
If somebody got more successful than me,

00:06:13.838 --> 00:06:16.004
it was partly my success.

00:06:16.004 --> 00:06:18.174
My success wasn't based on how

00:06:18.174 --> 00:06:20.624
I could push down everybody
that was around me.

00:06:20.624 --> 00:06:24.117
My success was based on how
much I could push everybody up.

00:06:24.117 --> 00:06:26.522
And eventually their
success was the same way,

00:06:26.522 --> 00:06:28.384
and in the process they pushed me up,

00:06:28.384 --> 00:06:30.500
and I pushed them up,
and we kept doing that.

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:31.685
And we still do that.

00:06:31.685 --> 00:06:33.778
And even though we all have

00:06:33.778 --> 00:06:36.728
in essence, competing companies,

00:06:36.728 --> 00:06:39.919
we see it as, if everybody succeeds,

00:06:39.919 --> 00:06:42.471
or my friend succeeds,
then everybody succeeds.

00:06:42.471 --> 00:06:44.565
And that's the key to it, is
to have everybody succeed,

00:06:44.565 --> 00:06:47.312
not to gloat over somebody else's failure.

00:06:47.312 --> 00:06:49.038
One of the basic motifs of fairy tales

00:06:49.038 --> 00:06:50.645
is that you find the poor, unfortunate

00:06:50.645 --> 00:06:52.028
along the side of the road,

00:06:52.028 --> 00:06:55.594
and when they beg for help,
if you give it to them,

00:06:55.594 --> 00:06:56.755
you end up succeeding.

00:06:56.755 --> 00:06:57.823
If you don't give it to them,

00:06:57.823 --> 00:07:01.935
you end up being turned
into a frog or something.

00:07:01.935 --> 00:07:03.872
It's something that's been
around for thousands of years,

00:07:03.872 --> 00:07:07.118
a concept that's been around
for thousands of years.

00:07:07.118 --> 00:07:09.580
It is even more necessary today,

00:07:09.580 --> 00:07:12.913
when people are much more into their own

00:07:13.766 --> 00:07:16.887
engrandizement, than they are in

00:07:16.887 --> 00:07:18.196
helping other people, I mean

00:07:18.196 --> 00:07:21.264
the one thing you hear
at the academy here,

00:07:21.264 --> 00:07:25.031
is constantly about public
service, about helping others.

00:07:25.031 --> 00:07:25.998
I don't think there's anybody who's

00:07:25.998 --> 00:07:28.917
become successful that doesn't understand

00:07:28.917 --> 00:07:30.892
how important it is to be part

00:07:30.892 --> 00:07:33.437
of a larger community,
to help other people

00:07:33.437 --> 00:07:35.970
in the larger community, to give back

00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:37.387
to the community.

00:07:38.273 --> 00:07:40.824
It's not something you do, you start doing

00:07:40.824 --> 00:07:41.905
when you've made it.

00:07:41.905 --> 00:07:45.079
Now I'm on the top, I can sort of,

00:07:45.079 --> 00:07:48.404
enhance my joy and self-esteem

00:07:48.404 --> 00:07:50.403
by helping the poor underlings.

00:07:50.403 --> 00:07:54.135
it's when you are at
the very lowest level,

00:07:54.135 --> 00:07:55.113
and you're struggling.

00:07:55.113 --> 00:07:56.712
When we were in film school and stuff,

00:07:56.712 --> 00:07:58.055
we were all very, very poor,

00:07:58.055 --> 00:07:59.292
we were all very, very struggling,

00:07:59.292 --> 00:08:01.874
we all need jobs very desperately,

00:08:01.874 --> 00:08:03.888
and if one of us couldn't
get a particular job,

00:08:03.888 --> 00:08:06.749
we'd send another friend
in on the interview.

00:08:06.749 --> 00:08:11.486
Because we were hoping that
one of us would get the job.

00:08:11.486 --> 00:08:13.905
So you do it right from
the very beginning.

00:08:13.905 --> 00:08:16.857
You can start every single day

00:08:16.857 --> 00:08:19.846
whether it's helping
your brother or sister,

00:08:19.846 --> 00:08:22.513
or helping your peers at school,

00:08:23.918 --> 00:08:25.303
or helping in the community,

00:08:25.303 --> 00:08:29.822
but it's not just a kind
of public service thing,

00:08:29.822 --> 00:08:30.984
it's a way of life.

00:08:30.984 --> 00:08:32.790
If America is the pursuit of happiness,

00:08:32.790 --> 00:08:35.374
the best way to pursue happiness
is to help other people.

00:08:35.374 --> 00:08:38.182
'Cause there's nothing else
that'll make you happy.

00:08:38.182 --> 00:08:40.413
You can be as rich and famous and powerful

00:08:40.413 --> 00:08:43.004
as you want to be, and it
will not bring you happiness.

00:08:43.004 --> 00:08:44.415
And that's said over
and over and over again,

00:08:44.415 --> 00:08:47.020
it's such a cliche, it hardly goes,

00:08:47.020 --> 00:08:49.052
it hardly needs to be said, but people

00:08:49.052 --> 00:08:52.469
don't understand that it's actually true.

00:08:53.329 --> 00:08:57.261
You can find people,
rich, powerful, and famous

00:08:57.261 --> 00:08:59.094
and they aren't happy.

00:09:01.052 --> 00:09:03.202
And you can find people
who have discovered

00:09:03.202 --> 00:09:05.238
the fact that it's really helping people.

00:09:05.238 --> 00:09:06.860
It's really being compassionate toward

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:10.572
other human beings that makes you happy.

00:09:10.572 --> 00:09:14.117
- At the risk of sounding like
a Miss America contestant,

00:09:14.117 --> 00:09:15.923
the most fulfilling
things I've experienced

00:09:15.923 --> 00:09:18.989
have truly been the human interactions.

00:09:18.989 --> 00:09:20.923
Spending time with women in village banks

00:09:20.923 --> 00:09:24.154
in Mexico with Think, a
microfinance organization.

00:09:24.154 --> 00:09:25.489
Meeting young women who were the first

00:09:25.489 --> 00:09:27.121
and only in their communities to attend

00:09:27.121 --> 00:09:29.211
secondary school in rural Kenya

00:09:29.211 --> 00:09:30.643
with Free the Children,
a group that builds

00:09:30.643 --> 00:09:33.721
sustainable schools in
developing countries.

00:09:33.721 --> 00:09:36.621
Trucking with guerrilla
conservationists in Rwanda.

00:09:36.621 --> 00:09:38.726
It's a cliche because it's true.

00:09:38.726 --> 00:09:41.762
That helping others ends up
helping you more than anyone.

00:09:41.762 --> 00:09:43.739
Getting out of your own concerns,

00:09:43.739 --> 00:09:46.313
and caring about someone
else's life for awhile,

00:09:46.313 --> 00:09:49.670
reminds you that you are not
the center of the universe.

00:09:49.670 --> 00:09:52.917
And that in the ways we
are generous, or not,

00:09:52.917 --> 00:09:55.285
we can change the course
of someone's life.

00:09:55.285 --> 00:09:57.631
Even at work, the small feats of kindness

00:09:57.631 --> 00:10:00.835
crew members, directors,
fellow actors have shown me

00:10:00.835 --> 00:10:02.879
have had the most lasting impact.

